{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018FQvY5KrA4fwuGxMvqXGny","type":"tool_result","content":"320\t- `check-active-hooks-executable.py` validates a hook's `type` and\n321\t  `matcher` the way the loader does. A settings path that exists but is\n322\t  not a regular file, and a filesystem error while resolving a hook path,\n323\t  exit 2 with a named error.\n324\t- `check_harness_config.py` turns an exception in that sub-check into a\n325\t  RED finding and keeps its output. Before, it was a degraded warning,\n326\t  which reads as green.\n327\t- A `claudeMdExcludes` value that excludes the root `CLAUDE.md` is\n328\t  classified as settings tamper (`settings_tamper_claude_md_excludes_root`).\n329\t- The harness gate adds an advisory finding, `root_instruction_load`. It\n330\t  reports `root_load_unobservable` unless you pass a complete list of the\n331\t  loaded instruction files with `--instruction-load-evidence`.\n332\t- The plan-lifecycle hook recognizes `PLAN-NNN-FOLLOWUP-<slug>.md` names\n333\t  (`_lib/plan_paths.py`).\n334\t- `check_cost_envelope.py` evaluates nothing, and records no spend, without\n335\t  an absolute `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`. Before, it fell back to the current\n336\t  directory.\n337\t\n338\t### Changed — `/ceo-boot` and other files that reach installs\n339\t\n340\t- `/ceo-boot` adds `main_checkout_state`. It is yellow, never red, when the\n341\t  main checkout is off its default branch or on a detached HEAD.\n342\t- The installer copies every top-level `.claude/scripts/*.py`, so six new\n343\t  maintenance helpers reach installs: `_contamination_inventory.py`,\n344\t  `_repin_guard.py`, `_restore_archive.py`, `_subprocess_census.py`,\n345\t  `caller_repo_guard.py` and `check-subprocess-tests.py`. `ceo-restore.sh`\n346\t  uses `_restore_archive.py`.\n347\t\n348\t### Changed — the framework's own CI and release (no installed file changes)\n349\t\n350\t- `scripts/install-npm.sh`, the local packaging check, reads the\n351\t  `npm pack --json` output of npm 12.2 and later, which keys the record by\n352\t  package name. Publishing still pins npm 11.20.0.\n353\t- CI runs on GitHub-hosted runners. The repository variable\n354\t  `VM_CI_ENABLED` is unset, so the self-hosted route that `[1.4.3]`\n355\t  describes is off (measured 2026-11-03).\n356\t- Release automation for the maintainer's Mac: `make mac-sign-merge`,\n357\t  `make mac-cut-rc` and `make mac-cut-ga`.\n358\t- Two red nightly runs on the merge of pull request #7 are cured:\n359\t  - The subprocess census fingerprint pins `ast.dump(..., show_empty=True)`\n360\t    on Python 3.13 and later, so a baseline recorded on 3.9 to 3.12 no\n361\t    longer reads as phantom sites on 3.13 and 3.14. The push run of\n362\t    `validate.yml` has no 3.14 leg; the scheduled run on that commit\n363\t    passed on 2026-11-03, its Python 3.14 leg included.\n364\t  - The ownership e2e check C.3 finds the canonical guards through the\n365\t    `--is-canonical` oracle, with a never-canonical negative control,\n366\t    instead of grepping the hook text.\n367\t- The Coverage job's timeout rises from 35 to 50 minutes. The scheduled\n368\t  job ran 34 min 36 s on 2026-11-02 and was cancelled at 35 min 05 s on\n369\t  2026-11-03, and a cancelled run on `HEAD` blocks the release preflight.\n370\t- `REVIEW.md` is added to the framework repository, and its existing\n371\t  pull-request template gains prompts for the literal output of the test\n372\t  plan, for the canonical or kernel files touched, and for how to revert.\n373\t\n374\t### Known-open (declared by this release)\n375\t\n376\t- **The Tier-1 coverage gate and the `mcp-smoke.yml` path filters still\n377\t  name only the entry points.** The coverage gate measures the entry-point\n378\t  files, not the packages that hold the logic. The `mcp-smoke.yml` push and\n379\t  pull-request filters name the entry points, not the split's packages;\n380\t  its daily scheduled run still runs. No release is assigned to its cure\n381\t  yet.\n382\t- **The pair-rail inputs-hash manifest lists the entry points only.** The\n383\t  inputs hash that the release workflow recomputes does not cover the\n384\t  packages behind them. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n385\t- **The VETO floor admits retired ids** (Security and governance, above).\n386\t  No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n387\t- **The model allowlist admits other vendors' ids and retired ids**\n388\t  (Security and governance, above). The settings-tamper detector\n389\t  (`settings_tamper_model_remap`), the agent `model:` check and the\n390\t  tier-policy loader no longer flag an id of the admitted families,\n391\t  Gemini, Grok and retired Claude 4 ids among them. No release is assigned\n392\t  to its cure yet.\n393\t- **The Codex pin.** A newer Codex CLI passes on its declared version\n394\t  alone, the pin file's floor and the ADR-182 amendment disagree, and the\n395\t  verdict validator does not bind a declared version to its payload hash.\n396\t  The hook fixtures for Codex as a hook host still date from 0.139.0. No\n397\t  release is assigned to its cure yet.\n398\t- **Files merged without the signing ceremony.** The 233 canonical paths\n399\t  that pull requests #6 and #7 brought, the kernel paths among them, are\n400\t  covered by no signed sentinel, and `main` has no protection that would\n401\t  prevent a repeat. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n402\t- **The audit spool.** The exit-drain change is partial: per-process files\n403\t  remain, part of the planned cure is pending, and its stress run is not\n404\t  complete. Whether every skipped event is collected later is not proven.\n405\t  No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n406\t- **The new Bash guard.** Its replay acceptance against the historical\n407\t  command corpus, the private vocabulary row and the OS-sandbox\n408\t  measurements did not run on this implementation. No release is assigned\n409\t  to its cure yet.\n410\t- **Backups and restore.** The key material (`audit-key`, `.salt`) is\n411\t  still not archived. A restore with no `.sha256` file proceeds with a\n412\t  warning. Without `--dest`, an applied restore overwrites the live audit\n413\t  logs while the chain-state files next to them stay as they were.\n414\t  Restore refuses `audit-key` and `.salt` in any letter case, but an\n415\t  HFS+ destination treats more names as equal than the default macOS\n416\t  disk (APFS) does, and there a crafted archive can still reach those\n417\t  files. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n418\t- **The redactor runs before parsing on the way in.** The ingress path\n419\t  redacts the raw answer text before parsing it, so a redaction can change\n420\t  whether an answer parses. This predates the redactor fix above. Its\n421\t  structural cure, redacting the decoded values after parsing, is scheduled\n422\t  for the next release.\n423\t\n424\t### Known-open (carried)\n425\t\n426\t- **The v1.4.0 annex.**\n427\t  - The signed v1.4.0 verdict carries an annex of P1 findings and declared\n428\t    conditions. v1.4.3-rc.1 retired signed condition 67, and that cure is\n429\t    part of this release.\n430\t  - Signed condition 58 is the redactor offsets defect fixed above.\n431\t  - This release does not claim the cure of any other annex condition. The\n432\t    partial spool change above is not claimed as one.\n433\t  - **The rest stays open, and no release is assigned to their cure.**\n434\t  - The list is in the sources the `[1.4.2]` entry names.\n435\t- **The v1.4.2 rc.1 annex.** This release does not claim the cure of its\n436\t  three P1 findings or of its P2 findings, declared in the `[1.4.3]` entry.\n437\t- **The rest of the `[1.4.3]` Known-open stays open as written there**,\n438\t  except where this release changes it:\n439\t  - the restore and strict-mode defects (above; the restore defects\n440\t    partly);\n441\t  - the harness sub-check gaps and the sub-check delegation (above; the\n442\t    sub-check partly);\n443\t  - the plan-lifecycle names and the cost-envelope directory (above);\n444\t  - the Bash guard's indirect-execution case (above, partly);\n445\t  - the root `CLAUDE.md` exclusion (above, partly);\n446\t  - the audit spool (above, partly);\n447\t  - the Codex pin and Codex as a hook host (Security and governance,\n448\t    above, partly): a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version now passes\n449\t    on its declared version without a re-pin, and the pin range, which\n450\t    admitted up to 0.160.x, has no upper bound.\n451\t- **Audit files can be deleted by Claude Code.** Claude Code's cleanup\n452\t  sweep unlinks every top-level `*.jsonl` in each project directory that is\n453\t  older than the `cleanupPeriodDays` of the session running the sweep\n454\t  (default 30), the audit logs and their rotated archives included. There\n455\t  is no plan in this release with a structural cure. The maintainer settings\n456\t  template still ships 90; the framework repository's own settings now use\n457\t  3650, and installs do not receive that file. The protection is still a\n458\t  scheduled backup with `ceo-backup.sh`.\n459\t\n460\t### What an adopter should know before installing or upgrading\n461\t\n462\t- Coming from v1.4.2, read `[1.4.3]` too. Its notes apply.\n463\t- The requirements are unchanged: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later, Python 3.9\n464\t  or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim.\n465\t- A command that reaches `rm -rf`, `git reset --hard` or a forced\n466\t  `git push` indirectly may now be blocked, and so may a shell body the\n467\t  guard cannot parse. To turn the scan off, set\n468\t  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0`, or only the heuristics with\n469\t  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0`, in the environment Claude Code starts\n470\t  with. Each disable is recorded once per session.\n471\t- If you patched a hook by hand, re-apply the patch to its module. The\n472\t  modules behind the seven kernel entry points, the five flat modules\n473\t  above and `_lib/adapters/live/` are kernel paths now: a sentinel alone\n474\t  no longer unlocks them; they need `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` with\n475\t  `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=I-ACCEPT`.\n476\t- If your code reads `Finding.start` and `Finding.end` from\n477\t  `secret_patterns.scan()`, they now index the text you passed in, not its\n478\t  NFKC form.\n479\t- Expect a yellow `main_checkout_state` in `/ceo-boot` when your main\n480\t  checkout is on a feature branch.\n481\t- `ceo-restore.sh` previews by default; pass `--apply` to restore. Keep your\n482\t  own copy of `audit-key` and `.salt`, because the backup leaves them out.\n483\t- The VETO floor now admits retired model ids. If your veto roles must not\n484\t  run on them, check the `model:` of your agent files yourself.\n485\t- The model allowlist now admits other vendors' ids (Gemini, Grok) and\n486\t  retired Claude 4 ids. `/ceo-boot` no longer reports a model variable set\n487\t  to one of them as `settings_tamper_model_remap`, and `validate-governance.sh`\n488\t  accepts such a `model:` in an agent file. If you rely on that tamper\n489\t  finding, check the model variables of your settings yourself.\n490\t- If you rely on the audit trail for longer than 30 days, raise\n491\t  `cleanupPeriodDays` at user scope and back up the audit files.\n492\t\n493\t## [1.4.3] - 2026-10-15\n494\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"129f718c-1dcf-49c9-b169-94f7b5847423","uuid":"644dbcb3-b334-4487-bfe8-534ec7581441","timestamp":"2026-11-15T09:27:04.432Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"- `check-active-hooks-executable.py` validates a hook's `type` and\n  `matcher` the way the loader does. A settings path that exists but is\n  not a regular file, and a filesystem error while resolving a hook path,\n  exit 2 with a named error.\n- `check_harness_config.py` turns an exception in that sub-check into a\n  RED finding and keeps its output. Before, it was a degraded warning,\n  which reads as green.\n- A `claudeMdExcludes` value that excludes the root `CLAUDE.md` is\n  classified as settings tamper (`settings_tamper_claude_md_excludes_root`).\n- The harness gate adds an advisory finding, `root_instruction_load`. It\n  reports `root_load_unobservable` unless you pass a complete list of the\n  loaded instruction files with `--instruction-load-evidence`.\n- The plan-lifecycle hook recognizes `PLAN-NNN-FOLLOWUP-<slug>.md` names\n  (`_lib/plan_paths.py`).\n- `check_cost_envelope.py` evaluates nothing, and records no spend, without\n  an absolute `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`. Before, it fell back to the current\n  directory.\n\n### Changed — `/ceo-boot` and other files that reach installs\n\n- `/ceo-boot` adds `main_checkout_state`. It is yellow, never red, when the\n  main checkout is off its default branch or on a detached HEAD.\n- The installer copies every top-level `.claude/scripts/*.py`, so six new\n  maintenance helpers reach installs: `_contamination_inventory.py`,\n  `_repin_guard.py`, `_restore_archive.py`, `_subprocess_census.py`,\n  `caller_repo_guard.py` and `check-subprocess-tests.py`. `ceo-restore.sh`\n  uses `_restore_archive.py`.\n\n### Changed — the framework's own CI and release (no installed file changes)\n\n- `scripts/install-npm.sh`, the local packaging check, reads the\n  `npm pack --json` output of npm 12.2 and later, which keys the record by\n  package name. Publishing still pins npm 11.20.0.\n- CI runs on GitHub-hosted runners. The repository variable\n  `VM_CI_ENABLED` is unset, so the self-hosted route that `[1.4.3]`\n  describes is off (measured 2026-11-03).\n- Release automation for the maintainer's Mac: `make mac-sign-merge`,\n  `make mac-cut-rc` and `make mac-cut-ga`.\n- Two red nightly runs on the merge of pull request #7 are cured:\n  - The subprocess census fingerprint pins `ast.dump(..., show_empty=True)`\n    on Python 3.13 and later, so a baseline recorded on 3.9 to 3.12 no\n    longer reads as phantom sites on 3.13 and 3.14. The push run of\n    `validate.yml` has no 3.14 leg; the scheduled run on that commit\n    passed on 2026-11-03, its Python 3.14 leg included.\n  - The ownership e2e check C.3 finds the canonical guards through the\n    `--is-canonical` oracle, with a never-canonical negative control,\n    instead of grepping the hook text.\n- The Coverage job's timeout rises from 35 to 50 minutes. The scheduled\n  job ran 34 min 36 s on 2026-11-02 and was cancelled at 35 min 05 s on\n  2026-11-03, and a cancelled run on `HEAD` blocks the release preflight.\n- `REVIEW.md` is added to the framework repository, and its existing\n  pull-request template gains prompts for the literal output of the test\n  plan, for the canonical or kernel files touched, and for how to revert.\n\n### Known-open (declared by this release)\n\n- **The Tier-1 coverage gate and the `mcp-smoke.yml` path filters still\n  name only the entry points.** The coverage gate measures the entry-point\n  files, not the packages that hold the logic. The `mcp-smoke.yml` push and\n  pull-request filters name the entry points, not the split's packages;\n  its daily scheduled run still runs. No release is assigned to its cure\n  yet.\n- **The pair-rail inputs-hash manifest lists the entry points only.** The\n  inputs hash that the release workflow recomputes does not cover the\n  packages behind them. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The VETO floor admits retired ids** (Security and governance, above).\n  No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The model allowlist admits other vendors' ids and retired ids**\n  (Security and governance, above). The settings-tamper detector\n  (`settings_tamper_model_remap`), the agent `model:` check and the\n  tier-policy loader no longer flag an id of the admitted families,\n  Gemini, Grok and retired Claude 4 ids among them. No release is assigned\n  to its cure yet.\n- **The Codex pin.** A newer Codex CLI passes on its declared version\n  alone, the pin file's floor and the ADR-182 amendment disagree, and the\n  verdict validator does not bind a declared version to its payload hash.\n  The hook fixtures for Codex as a hook host still date from 0.139.0. No\n  release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **Files merged without the signing ceremony.** The 233 canonical paths\n  that pull requests #6 and #7 brought, the kernel paths among them, are\n  covered by no signed sentinel, and `main` has no protection that would\n  prevent a repeat. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The audit spool.** The exit-drain change is partial: per-process files\n  remain, part of the planned cure is pending, and its stress run is not\n  complete. Whether every skipped event is collected later is not proven.\n  No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The new Bash guard.** Its replay acceptance against the historical\n  command corpus, the private vocabulary row and the OS-sandbox\n  measurements did not run on this implementation. No release is assigned\n  to its cure yet.\n- **Backups and restore.** The key material (`audit-key`, `.salt`) is\n  still not archived. A restore with no `.sha256` file proceeds with a\n  warning. Without `--dest`, an applied restore overwrites the live audit\n  logs while the chain-state files next to them stay as they were.\n  Restore refuses `audit-key` and `.salt` in any letter case, but an\n  HFS+ destination treats more names as equal than the default macOS\n  disk (APFS) does, and there a crafted archive can still reach those\n  files. No release is assigned to its cure yet.\n- **The redactor runs before parsing on the way in.** The ingress path\n  redacts the raw answer text before parsing it, so a redaction can change\n  whether an answer parses. This predates the redactor fix above. Its\n  structural cure, redacting the decoded values after parsing, is scheduled\n  for the next release.\n\n### Known-open (carried)\n\n- **The v1.4.0 annex.**\n  - The signed v1.4.0 verdict carries an annex of P1 findings and declared\n    conditions. v1.4.3-rc.1 retired signed condition 67, and that cure is\n    part of this release.\n  - Signed condition 58 is the redactor offsets defect fixed above.\n  - This release does not claim the cure of any other annex condition. The\n    partial spool change above is not claimed as one.\n  - **The rest stays open, and no release is assigned to their cure.**\n  - The list is in the sources the `[1.4.2]` entry names.\n- **The v1.4.2 rc.1 annex.** This release does not claim the cure of its\n  three P1 findings or of its P2 findings, declared in the `[1.4.3]` entry.\n- **The rest of the `[1.4.3]` Known-open stays open as written there**,\n  except where this release changes it:\n  - the restore and strict-mode defects (above; the restore defects\n    partly);\n  - the harness sub-check gaps and the sub-check delegation (above; the\n    sub-check partly);\n  - the plan-lifecycle names and the cost-envelope directory (above);\n  - the Bash guard's indirect-execution case (above, partly);\n  - the root `CLAUDE.md` exclusion (above, partly);\n  - the audit spool (above, partly);\n  - the Codex pin and Codex as a hook host (Security and governance,\n    above, partly): a Codex CLI newer than the manifest version now passes\n    on its declared version without a re-pin, and the pin range, which\n    admitted up to 0.160.x, has no upper bound.\n- **Audit files can be deleted by Claude Code.** Claude Code's cleanup\n  sweep unlinks every top-level `*.jsonl` in each project directory that is\n  older than the `cleanupPeriodDays` of the session running the sweep\n  (default 30), the audit logs and their rotated archives included. There\n  is no plan in this release with a structural cure. The maintainer settings\n  template still ships 90; the framework repository's own settings now use\n  3650, and installs do not receive that file. The protection is still a\n  scheduled backup with `ceo-backup.sh`.\n\n### What an adopter should know before installing or upgrading\n\n- Coming from v1.4.2, read `[1.4.3]` too. Its notes apply.\n- The requirements are unchanged: Claude Code 2.1.280 or later, Python 3.9\n  or later, and Node.js 20 or later for the npm shim.\n- A command that reaches `rm -rf`, `git reset --hard` or a forced\n  `git push` indirectly may now be blocked, and so may a shell body the\n  guard cannot parse. To turn the scan off, set\n  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0`, or only the heuristics with\n  `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0`, in the environment Claude Code starts\n  with. Each disable is recorded once per session.\n- If you patched a hook by hand, re-apply the patch to its module. The\n  modules behind the seven kernel entry points, the five flat modules\n  above and `_lib/adapters/live/` are kernel paths now: a sentinel alone\n  no longer unlocks them; they need `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` with\n  `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=I-ACCEPT`.\n- If your code reads `Finding.start` and `Finding.end` from\n  `secret_patterns.scan()`, they now index the text you passed in, not its\n  NFKC form.\n- Expect a yellow `main_checkout_state` in `/ceo-boot` when your main\n  checkout is on a feature branch.\n- `ceo-restore.sh` previews by default; pass `--apply` to restore. Keep your\n  own copy of `audit-key` and `.salt`, because the backup leaves them out.\n- The VETO floor now admits retired model ids. If your veto roles must not\n  run on them, check the `model:` of your agent files yourself.\n- The model allowlist now admits other vendors' ids (Gemini, Grok) and\n  retired Claude 4 ids. `/ceo-boot` no longer reports a model variable set\n  to one of them as `settings_tamper_model_remap`, and `validate-governance.sh`\n  accepts such a `model:` in an agent file. If you rely on that tamper\n  finding, check the model variables of your settings yourself.\n- If you rely on the audit trail for longer than 30 days, raise\n  `cleanupPeriodDays` at user scope and back up the audit files.\n\n## [1.4.3] - 2026-10-15\n","numLines":175,"startLine":320,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018FQvY5KrA4fwuGxMvqXGny","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
